          "title": "Discrete Vernier Fixed-Point Contractive Invariance",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Discrete Vernier Fixed-Point Contractive Invariance\n\n[#theorem-vernier-fixed-point]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Discrete Vernier Fixed-Point Invariance Theorem\n====\nLet \\( f : \\mathcal { G } _ N \\to \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) be a contractive mapping on finite rational grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) satisfying \\( d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( f ( x ) , f ( y ) ) \\le \\gamma d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( x , y ) \\) with rational contraction constant \\( \\gamma < 1 \\). Then \\( f \\) possesses a unique fixed point \\( x ^ \\ast \\in \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) reached in a finite number of iterative evaluation steps \\( M \\le \\lceil \\log _ \\gamma ( \\delta / \\text { diam } ( \\mathcal { G } _ N ) ) \\rceil \\).\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Iterative Distance Reduction**: For initial point \\( x _ 0 \\in \\mathcal { G } _ N \\), generate iteration sequence \\( x _ { k + 1 } = f ( x _ k ) \\). The metric distance satisfies \\( d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( x _ { k + 1 } , x _ k ) \\le \\gamma ^ k d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( x _ 1 , x _ 0 ) \\).\n\n. **Finite Resolution Threshold**: Since all non-zero distances on \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) are bounded below by grid step resolution \\( \\delta = 1 / N \\), there exists finite integer \\( M \\) such that \\( \\gamma ^ M d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( x _ 1 , x _ 0 ) < \\delta \\).\n\n. **Exact Fixed Point Invariance**: Therefore, \\( d _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( x _ { M + 1 } , x _ M ) = 0 \\), proving \\( x _ { M + 1 } = x _ M = x ^ \\ast \\). Uniqueness follows directly from contraction inequality. \\( \\square \\)\n===="
